## About these conditions

**Respiratory** conditions affect your lungs and airways. Lung conditions affect around 1 in 5 people in the UK at some point. Allergies, such as hay fever, happen when your immune system reacts to something that is usually harmless.

## Help that applies to many conditions

- **Benefits are based on how a condition affects you,** not on its name. If you need help with everyday tasks or getting around, try our [PIP points self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/), or the [Attendance Allowance check](/benefits/attendance-allowance/checker/) if you are over State Pension age.
- **If you cannot work because of your health,** read about [New Style ESA](/benefits/esa/) and the [Universal Credit health element](/benefits/universal-credit-health/).
- **You may be protected at work.** Under the Equality Act 2010, a condition that has a substantial, long-term effect on your daily life can count as a disability, which means your employer must consider reasonable adjustments.
- **Help with health costs.** Some conditions give you free prescriptions in England, and prescriptions are free for everyone in Wales. Try our [NHS health costs check](/support/health-costs/checker/).
- **Your GP** can review your treatment, refer you to specialists and tell you about local support.

{% callout type="danger" title="In an emergency" %}
Call **999** if someone is struggling to breathe, cannot speak in full sentences, has blue or grey lips, or has swelling of the lips, tongue or throat. These can be signs of a severe asthma attack or anaphylaxis. For urgent medical help that is not an emergency, call **NHS 111**.
{% /callout %}
